## Co-evolve environment and exploit
- The environment stage encoded runtime hypotheses (libc series, loader,
  protections) from writeup clues. Your exploit is the test of those hypotheses.
- When the R5 `cage-check` replay shows a runtime-shaped contradiction — a leaked libc
  address inconsistent with the chosen glibc, a wrong loader, a protection the
  writeup implies but the runtime lacks — feed it back and fix the ENVIRONMENT
  files, then re-run. When the observation is a logic slip against a runtime the
  writeup corroborates, fix the exploit instead.
- Break ties about which side to move with the writeup's own concrete clues
  (named Ubuntu/glibc, libc build-id, allocator notes), not by guessing.

## Lower confidence, honest unresolved
- Confidence is lower than a build-anchored route; do not overclaim a pass.
- If the writeup underspecifies the runtime or a solve step so the pair cannot
  be closed from the supplied evidence, stop and emit `unresolved` naming the
  last confirmed milestone and the exact underspecified detail. Do not fabricate
  the runtime, the primitive, or the flag.
